  "notes": "The Lower Broadway hillbilly revival — a bar band off a nightly residency at Robert's Western World that took honky-tonk, rockabilly, western swing and Bakersfield to a major label in one set. The drum block is the best-documented part of the entry: a small kit with \"the triplet lope on a tight hat, shuffled two-beat kick … brushes on the weepers; quarter kick on the swing numbers\", and the constraint that the drummer also sings backing vocals, which the research says \"caps pattern complexity — model low fill density\". That is authored as fillDensity 0.24, an open hat at 0.10 and fills pushed out to 8/16 bars, against the genre's 0.5, 0.18 and 4/8. Upright bass with slap-style percussive accents is \"a standing feature — on this band the slap is a second snare\", so the bass sits low (26–45) and follows roots at 0.84 with walking excursions rather than running lines. The harmonic palette is authored as the entry frames it — a *period* rather than a style, \"when honky tonk, rockabilly, Western swing, bittersweet storyteller swill, and Sheb Wooley all commingled\" — so twelve-bar frames, I–IV–V with V7, I–I7–IV and the western-swing ii–V all sit in one weighted set. The multi-instrumentalist constraint (one player covering fiddle, steel and mandolin, so only one of those colours can sound at a time) is why the counter rotates three styles at a moderate density rather than layering. ⚠ ALL tempo numbers here are INFERRED: the research states plainly that \"no per-track readings were reachable\" and that the 100–190 band was itself inferred from the archetype's three tempo families, so bpm.mode 128 is this author's reading of the mid-to-fast centre of that band and is doubly [LOW-CONF]. ⚠ Also INFERRED: swing.amount 0.63, all velocity tiers, ghost probability and vel, and the percs density. Confidence is medium exactly as the research rates it.",
